    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d849">

                <pb facs="1089" n="987" sameAs="frus:frus1935v01/pg987"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">681.003/187</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Belgium</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Sussdorff</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 473</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Brussels</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1935-05-23">May 23,
                        1935</date>.<lb/>[Received June 5.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to refer to the
                    Department’s instructions Nos. 190 of August 9, 1934<anchor n="79"
                        xml:id="d849-f1"/> and 173 of February 12, 1935,<anchor n="80"
                        xml:id="d849-f2"/> and to the Embassy’s despatches No. 246 of September 4,
                        1934,<anchor n="81" xml:id="d849-f3"/> and No. 393 of February 28,
                        1935,<anchor n="82" xml:id="d849-f4"/> regarding a proposed modification of
                    the customs regime now in effect in the French Protectorate of Morocco.</p>
                <p>In this connection, I have the honor to report that, in reply to an inquiry, Mr.
                    Le Tellier, Chief of the Political Section of the Belgian Foreign Office
                    informed me today that the French Government has not made any representations to
                    the Belgian Government for a long time on the subject of the proposed
                    modifications. Mr. Le Tellier believes that such strong opposition developed on
                    various sides to the French proposals that the French Government feels that it
                    would be useless to push the matter at the present time. Mr. Le Tellier repeated
                    that the Belgian Government is strenuously opposed to the modifications proposed
                    by the French Government, as are a number of other countries. He said that the
                    opposition of the foreign countries to the French proposal was strengthened by
                    the fact that opposition also developed in Morocco on the part of a number of
                    important <pb facs="1090" n="988" xml:id="pg988"/> commercial interests. He
                    believes that the sum total of the opposition will be that the French Government
                    will allow the matter to rest for some time to come.</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Louis Sussdorff, Jr.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d849-f1" n="79" xml:id="d849-f1-content">
                <p>See instruction No. 505, August 3, 1934, to the Ambassador in Great Britain, <ref
                        target="frus1934v02#pg_844">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1934, vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">ii</hi>, p. 844</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d849-f2" n="80" xml:id="d849-f2-content">
                <p>Transmitting memorandum of January 24, 1935, by the Chief of the Division of
                    Western European Affairs, <ref target="#pg961">p. 961</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d849-f3" n="81" xml:id="d849-f3-content">
                <p>
                    <ref target="frus1934v02#pg_849">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1934, vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">ii</hi>, p. 849</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d849-f4" n="82" xml:id="d849-f4-content">
                <p>Not printed; it reported “the Belgian Government still opposed in principle to
                    the proposed changes, which would involve the establishment of a number of
                    quotas in Morocco.” (681.003/163)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
